\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"rdi\",\"source\":\"caller\",\"description\":\"error message string\"}],\"checks\":[],\"memory_operations\":[],\"calls\":[\"printf\",\"exit\"]},{\"name\":\"init\",\"address\":\"0x1232\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"setvbuf\",\"alarm\",\"fopen\",\"_abort\",\"strdup\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[],\"checks\":[\"urandom != NULL\"],\"memory_operations\":[{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"urandom\",\"address\":\"0x41e0\",\"size\":8,\"source\":\"fopen return\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg[0]\",\"address\":\"0x4060\",\"size\":8,\"source\":\"strdup return\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg_size[0]\",\"address\":\"0x4160\",\"size\":4,\"value\":\"0x50\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg[1]\",\"address\":\"0x4068\",\"size\":8,\"source\":\"strdup return\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg_size[1]\",\"address\":\"0x4164\",\"size\":4,\"value\":\"0x30\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg[2]\",\"address\":\"0x4070\",\"size\":8,\"source\":\"strdup return\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg_size[2]\",\"address\":\"0x4168\",\"size\":4,\"value\":\"0x18\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg[3]\",\"address\":\"0x4078\",\"size\":8,\"source\":\"strdup return\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg_size[3]\",\"address\":\"0x416c\",\"size\":4,\"value\":\"0x38\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg[4]\",\"address\":\"0x4080\",\"size\":8,\"source\":\"strdup return\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg_size[4]\",\"address\":\"0x4170\",\"size\":4,\"value\":\"0x40\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"cookie_num\",\"address\":\"0x41e8\",\"size\":8,\"value\":\"5\"}],\"calls\":[\"setvbuf\",\"alarm\",\"fopen\",\"strdup\"]},{\"name\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1399\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"printf\",\"_abort\",\"__isoc99_scanf\",\"calloc\",\"read\",\"puts\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"message_length\",\"source\":\"scanf(%lld)\",\"type\":\"long_long\"},{\"name\":\"message_data\",\"source\":\"read(0, buf, message_length)\",\"type\":\"raw_bytes\"}],\"checks\":[\"cookie_num <= 0x1f (31)\",\"message_length <= 0x100 (256)\"],\"memory_operations\":[{\"operation\":\"ALLOC\",\"function\":\"calloc\",\"size\":\"message_length\",\"destination\":\"stack_local [rbp-0x10]\"},{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"stdin\",\"destination\":\"calloc_buffer\",\"size\":\"message_length\",\"function\":\"read\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg[cookie_num]\",\"address\":\"0x4060 + cookie_num*8\",\"size\":8,\"source\":\"calloc_buffer\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg_size[cookie_num]\",\"address\":\"0x4160 + cookie_num*4\",\"size\":4,\"source\":\"message_length (truncated to int)\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"cookie_num\",\"address\":\"0x41e8\",\"size\":8,\"operation_detail\":\"cookie_num += 1\"}],\"calls\":[\"printf\",\"__isoc99_scanf\",\"calloc\",\"read\",\"puts\"]},{\"name\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x14e1\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"_abort\",\"fread\",\"printf\",\"free\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"random_byte\",\"source\":\"fread(urandom, 1, 1)\",\"type\":\"char\"}],\"checks\":[\"cookie_num != 0\"],\"memory_operations\":[{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"urandom\",\"destination\":\"stack_local [rbp-0xc]\",\"size\":1,\"function\":\"fread\"},{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"msg[random_byte % cookie_num]\",\"address\":\"0x4060 + (random_byte % cookie_num)*8\",\"size\":8,\"description\":\"load pointer for printf\"},{\"operation\":\"FREE\",\"pointer\":\"msg[random_byte % cookie_num]\",\"function\":\"free\"},{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"msg[cookie_num-1]\",\"address\":\"0x4060 + (cookie_num-1)*8\",\"size\":8,\"description\":\"load last element\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg[random_byte % cookie_num]\",\"address\":\"0x4060 + (random_byte % cookie_num)*8\",\"size\":8,\"source\":\"msg[cookie_num-1] value\",\"description\":\"move last pointer into freed slot\"},{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"msg_size[cookie_num-1]\",\"address\":\"0x4160 + (cookie_num-1)*4\",\"size\":4},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg_size[random_byte % cookie_num]\",\"address\":\"0x4160 + (random_byte % cookie_num)*4\",\"size\":4,\"source\":\"msg_size[cookie_num-1] value\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"cookie_num\",\"address\":\"0x41e8\",\"size\":8,\"operation_detail\":\"cookie_num -= 1\"}],\"calls\":[\"fread\",\"printf\",\"free\"]},{\"name\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1628\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"printf\",\"__isoc99_scanf\",\"_abort\",\"read\",\"puts\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"idx\",\"source\":\"scanf(%llu)\",\"type\":\"unsigned_long_long\"},{\"name\":\"new_message\",\"source\":\"read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1)\",\"type\":\"raw_bytes\"}],\"checks\":[\"idx < cookie_num (unsigned comparison)\"],\"memory_operations\":[{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"msg_size[idx]\",\"address\":\"0x4160 + idx*4\",\"size\":4,\"description\":\"load size for read limit\"},{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"msg[idx]\",\"address\":\"0x4060 + idx*8\",\"size\":8,\"description\":\"load heap pointer\"},{\"operation\":\"WRITE\",\"destination\":\"msg[idx] heap buffer\",\"size\":\"msg_size[idx]-1\",\"source\":\"stdin\",\"function\":\"read\"}],\"calls\":[\"printf\",\"__isoc99_scanf\",\"read\",\"puts\"]},{\"name\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1751\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"printf\",\"__isoc99_scanf\",\"_abort\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"idx\",\"source\":\"scanf(%llu)\",\"type\":\"unsigned_long_long\"}],\"checks\":[\"idx < cookie_num (unsigned comparison)\"],\"memory_operations\":[{\"operation\":\"READ\",\"source\":\"msg[idx]\",\"address\":\"0x4060 + idx*8\",\"size\":8,\"description\":\"load heap pointer\"},{\"operation\":\"OUTPUT\",\"source\":\"msg[idx]\",\"function\":\"printf\",\"format\":\"%s\"}],\"calls\":[\"printf\",\"__isoc99_scanf\"]},{\"name\":\"menu\",\"address\":\"0x17fd\",\"callers\":[\"main\"],\"callees\":[\"puts\",\"__stack_chk_fail\"],\"inputs\":[],\"checks\":[],\"memory_operations\":[],\"calls\":[\"puts\"]},{\"name\":\"main\",\"address\":\"0x187f\",\"callers\":[\"_start\"],\"callees\":[\"init\",\"menu\",\"__isoc99_scanf\",\"eat_cookie\",\"create_cookie\",\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\",\"exit\"],\"inputs\":[{\"name\":\"choice\",\"source\":\"scanf(%lld)\",\"type\":\"long_long\"}],\"checks\":[\"choice == 1: eat_cookie\",\"choice == 2: create_cookie\",\"choice == 3: edit_cookie\",\"choice == 4: read_cookie\",\"choice == 5: exit\"],\"memory_operations\":[],\"calls\":[\"init\",\"menu\",\"__isoc99_scanf\",\"eat_cookie\",\"create_cookie\",\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\",\"exit\"]}],\"attack_surfaces\":[{\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"input\":\"idx (scanf %llu, unsigned)\",\"operation\":\"WRITE to msg[idx] heap buffer via read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1)\",\"risk\":\"HIGH\",\"reason\":\"attacker-controlled unsigned idx indexes into msg[] pointer array and msg_size[] array; check is idx < cookie_num (unsigned); idx is used to compute both the heap pointer load and the size for read, writing attacker data into the heap buffer\"},{\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"input\":\"idx (scanf %llu, unsigned)\",\"operation\":\"READ msg[idx] and OUTPUT via printf(%s)\",\"risk\":\"HIGH\",\"reason\":\"attacker-controlled unsigned idx indexes into msg[] pointer array; check is idx < cookie_num (unsigned); the loaded pointer is passed to printf as a string, potentially leaking heap content\"},{\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"input\":\"random_byte from /dev/urandom\",\"operation\":\"FREE msg[random_byte % cookie_num] and compaction (move last element into freed slot)\",\"risk\":\"MEDIUM\",\"reason\":\"index is from urandom (not attacker-controlled), but the free+compaction sequence creates a pointer-management pattern where msg[last] is moved into the freed slot without clearing msg[last]; S2 should analyze whether this creates a dangling pointer or double-free scenario when the same slot is reused\"},{\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"input\":\"message_length (scanf %lld, signed)\",\"operation\":\"ALLOC calloc(message_length) and WRITE read(0, buf, message_length)\",\"risk\":\"MEDIUM\",\"reason\":\"attacker-controlled size (signed long long, checked <= 0x100); size is stored as int in msg_size[] (truncation from 8-byte to 4-byte); S2 should verify whether negative or large values bypass the check or cause truncation issues\"},{\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"input\":\"idx (scanf %llu)\",\"operation\":\"READ msg_size[idx] to compute read size\",\"risk\":\"MEDIUM\",\"reason\":\"attacker-controlled idx also indexes into msg_size[] (int array at 0x4160); if idx is within bounds of msg[] (32 entries) but the relationship between msg and msg_size arrays could be exploited, S2 should verify\"}],\"facts\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"Binary is ELF64 x86-64 PIE, dynamically linked, not stripped, compiled with GCC 10.2.0\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F002\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"Full RELRO, NX enabled, Canary found, PIE enabled; no FORTIFY, no CET (IBT/SHSTK) detected\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F003\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"libc is Ubuntu GLIBC 2.39-0ubuntu8.8 at /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6\",\"source\":\"RUNTIME\"},{\"id\":\"F004\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg is a pointer array at 0x4060, 32 elements of 8 bytes each (256 bytes total), in .bss\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F005\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg_size is an int array at 0x4160, 32 elements of 4 bytes each (128 bytes total), in .bss\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F006\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"cookie_num is a long at 0x41e8 in .bss, initialized to 5 in init()\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F007\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"urandom is a FILE* at 0x41e0, opened from /dev/urandom in init()\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F008\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"init() pre-creates 5 cookies via strdup with sizes 0x50, 0x30, 0x18, 0x38, 0x40\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F009\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"create_cookie checks cookie_num <= 31 and message_length <= 256 (both signed comparisons)\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F010\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"create_cookie stores message_length as int (4 bytes) in msg_size[] via mov ecx,edx, truncating from 8-byte long long\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F011\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie reads idx via scanf(%llu) as unsigned long long, checks idx < cookie_num (unsigned jl)\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F012\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie reads into msg[idx] buffer with size msg_size[idx]-1 via read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1)\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F013\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"read_cookie reads idx via scanf(%llu) as unsigned long long, checks idx < cookie_num (unsigned jl), then printf(%s, msg[idx])\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F014\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie selects a random index via fread(urandom, 1 byte) % cookie_num, frees msg[idx], then moves msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[idx] and decrements cookie_num\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F015\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie does NOT null out msg[cookie_num-1] after moving its pointer to msg[idx]\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F016\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"alarm(60) is called in init(), setting a 60-second timeout\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F017\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"main loop: calls menu(), scanf(%lld) for choice, dispatches to eat/create/edit/read/exit\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F018\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg array (0x4060, 256 bytes) and msg_size array (0x4160, 128 bytes) are contiguous in .bss with no gap between them\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F019\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"msg_size[-1] relative to msg_size base would be at 0x415c, which is still within the msg array (0x4060..0x4160), specifically msg[0x415c-0x4060] = msg[0xfc] = msg[252] bytes offset = element 31.5 — this means negative indexing of msg_size overlaps with msg pointer entries\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F020\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie and read_cookie use unsigned comparison (jl after cmp rdx,rax) for idx < cookie_num, meaning negative values wrapped as unsigned could pass the check if cookie_num is large enough\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F021\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"The GOT is at 0x3f58 with Full RELRO, meaning GOT entries are read-only at runtime\",\"source\":\"ELF\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"id\":\"U001\",\"description\":\"Whether ASLR is enabled at runtime\",\"reason\":\"Cannot confirm from ELF alone; requires runtime observation of address randomization\"},{\"id\":\"U002\",\"description\":\"Whether seccomp or other sandbox restrictions are in place\",\"reason\":\"No seccomp filter detected in ELF, but runtime restrictions cannot be ruled out without execution environment inspection\"},{\"id\":\"U003\",\"description\":\"Whether the unsigned idx comparison in edit_cookie/read_cookie allows out-of-bounds access when cookie_num is small (e.g., idx=0 with cookie_num=5 passes, but large unsigned values that wrap could also pass)\",\"reason\":\"The comparison is unsigned jl (idx < cookie_num); if idx is a very large unsigned value and cookie_num is small, it would fail; but the exact behavior depends on runtime cookie_num value and attacker-provided idx\"},{\"id\":\"U004\",\"description\":\"Whether eat_cookie's compaction (moving msg[last] to msg[freed_idx] without clearing msg[last]) creates a dangling pointer that could be exploited\",\"reason\":\"S2 needs to analyze the control flow: if cookie_num is decremented, msg[last] is logically out-of-bounds, but the stale pointer value remains in memory; whether this is reachable depends on subsequent operations\"},{\"id\":\"U005\",\"description\":\"Whether the truncation of message_length from long long (8 bytes) to int (4 bytes) in create_cookie creates a type confusion or size mismatch\",\"reason\":\"scanf reads %lld (8 bytes) but msg_size stores only 4 bytes (int); if message_length fits in int this is benign, but S2 should verify edge cases\"}],\"raw\":{\"elf\":\"ELF64 x86-64 PIE, dynamically linked, interpreter /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2, not stripped, GCC 10.2.0, entry 0x1110\",\"sections\":\"29 sections; .text@0x1110(0x891 AX), .rodata@0x2000(0x266 A), .data@0x4000(0x10 WA), .bss@0x4020(0x1d0 WA), .got@0x3f58(0xa8 WA), .plt@0x1020(0xe0 AX)\",\"symbols\":\"Key symbols: _abort@0x11f5, init@0x1232, create_cookie@0x1399, eat_cookie@0x14e1, edit_cookie@0x1628, read_cookie@0x1751, menu@0x17fd, main@0x187f; Globals: msg@0x4060(256B), msg_size@0x4160(128B), urandom@0x41e0(8B), cookie_num@0x41e8(8B)\",\"disassembly\":\"main: loop(menu->scanf(%lld)->dispatch 1-5); init: setvbuf*3, alarm(60), fopen(/dev/urandom), strdup*5 for initial cookies, cookie_num=5; create_cookie: check cookie_num<=31, scanf(%lld) size<=256, calloc(size), read(0,buf,size), store in msg[msg_num]/msg_size[cookie_num], cookie_num++; eat_cookie: fread(urandom,1,1), idx=byte%cookie_num, printf(msg[idx]), free(msg[idx]), msg[idx]=msg[cookie_num-1], msg_size[idx]=msg_size[cookie_num-1], cookie_num--; edit_cookie: printf range [0-cookie_num-1], scanf(%llu) idx, check idx<cookie_num, read(0,msg[idx],msg_size[idx]-1); read_cookie: scanf(%llu) idx, check idx<cookie_num, printf(%s,msg[idx])\"}},\"vulnerability_report\":{\"schema\":\"VulnerabilityReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"total_bugs\":3,\"high_severity\":2,\"medium_severity\":1,\"low_severity\":0},\"vulnerabilities\":[{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"title\":\"OOB Signedness Mismatch in edit_cookie and read_cookie (idx read as unsigned but checked with signed comparison)\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Signedness Error\",\"description\":\"idx is read via scanf(%llu) as unsigned long long, but the bounds check uses 'jl' (signed less-than jump) after 'cmp rdx, rax'. When idx is a very large unsigned value (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF = -1 signed), the signed comparison treats it as negative, passing the check (idx < cookie_num). The idx value is then used as an unsigned 64-bit offset to index into msg[] (8-byte stride) and msg_size[] (4-byte stride), causing out-of-bounds access to memory before the arrays.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg (pointer array at 0x4060, 32 elements)\",\"msg_size (int array at 0x4160, 32 elements)\",\"stdout (FILE* at 0x4020)\",\"stdin (FILE* at 0x4030)\",\"stderr (FILE* at 0x4040)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"cookie_index via scanf(%llu) in edit_cookie and read_cookie\",\"entry_function\":\"edit_cookie / read_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"Attacker provides idx via scanf(%llu) as unsigned long long\",\"idx stored in [rbp-0x10] (8 bytes)\",\"cmp rdx, rax compares idx (rdx) against cookie_num (rax)\",\"jl (signed less-than) passes when idx is a large unsigned value interpreted as negative\",\"idx used in lea rdx, [rax*8+0x0] to compute msg[idx] address (unsigned multiplication)\",\"msg[idx] pointer loaded from OOB memory\",\"edit_cookie: read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1) writes attacker data to OOB pointer target\",\"read_cookie: printf(%s, msg[idx]) reads and outputs data from OOB pointer target\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"idx should be checked with unsigned comparison (jb/jae) to ensure idx < cookie_num for unsigned values\",\"actual\":\"idx is checked with jl (signed less-than), allowing large unsigned values (interpreted as negative signed) to pass the check\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Incorrect comparison instruction: jl (signed) instead of jb (unsigned). The format specifier %llu reads unsigned, but the comparison treats the value as signed.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"OOB array access and pointer dereference\",\"address\":\"0x1681 (edit_cookie cmp), 0x17aa (read_cookie cmp), 0x16c3/16d2 (edit_cookie msg[idx] access), 0x17bf/17ce (read_cookie msg[idx] access)\",\"expression\":\"msg[idx] where idx is attacker-controlled unsigned value that bypassed signed comparison; edit_cookie: read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1); read_cookie: printf(%s, msg[idx])\",\"target\":\"msg[] array (0x4060) and msg_size[] array (0x4160) accessed out-of-bounds with negative indices; msg[-8] = stdout FILE* (0x4020), msg[-4] = stderr FILE* (0x4040)\",\"size\":\"msg_size[idx]-1 for edit_cookie (when msg_size[idx]=0, size becomes 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF via movsxd sign-extension)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"read_cookie with negative idx loads an OOB pointer from msg[] (e.g., stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* pointers) and passes it to printf(%s), leaking memory content at that address. Dynamically verified: idx=-8 caused printf to output binary data from the stdout FILE structure.\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"edit_cookie with negative idx loads an OOB pointer from msg[] (e.g., stdout FILE* at 0x4020) and calls read(0, OOB_pointer, msg_size[idx]-1). When msg_size[idx]=0, the size becomes 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (sign-extended -1), allowing a massive write to the OOB pointer target. Dynamically verified: idx=-8 caused read() to write attacker data to the stdout FILE structure address.\"},{\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"read_cookie with negative idx prints data from an OOB pointer via printf(%s), potentially leaking libc/heap addresses stored in global variables like stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* pointers.\"},{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"edit_cookie writes attacker-controlled data to arbitrary addresses pointed to by OOB msg[] entries, corrupting memory at those addresses (e.g., FILE structures, other global data).\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1681-0x1684\",\"description\":\"idx (rdx, from scanf %llu) is compared against cookie_num (rax) with 'cmp rdx, rax; jl 0x1692'. jl is a signed comparison, allowing large unsigned idx values (interpreted as negative signed) to pass the check.\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17aa-0x17ad\",\"description\":\"Same pattern: 'cmp rdx, rax; jl 0x17bb'. idx from scanf(%llu) checked with signed jl.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16a3-0x16d2\",\"description\":\"After the check passes, idx is used unsigned: 'mov rax, [rbp-0x10]; lea rdx, [rax*4+0x0]' for msg_size[idx] and 'lea rcx, [rax*8+0x0]' for msg[idx]. The unsigned multiplication produces OOB addresses for negative idx values.\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16b6-0x16de\",\"description\":\"msg_size[idx] is loaded, decremented by 1, and sign-extended via movsxd to 64-bit for read() size. When msg_size[idx]=0, size becomes 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF.\"},{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1684\",\"description\":\"GDB breakpoint at 0x1684: with input idx=18446744073709551608 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8 = -8 signed), rdx=0xfffffffffffffff8, rax=0x5. The jl check passed (-8 < 5 signed). Program proceeded to 'New Message: Done!' without crashing, confirming OOB access succeeded.\"},{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17ad\",\"description\":\"GDB breakpoint at 0x17ad: with input idx=18446744073709551608 (-8 signed), rdx=0xfffffffffffffff8, rax=0x5. The jl check passed. printf output binary data '(­û' from the stdout FILE structure, confirming OOB read and info leak.\"},{\"id\":\"E007\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16b6\",\"description\":\"GDB at 0x16b6: with idx=-8, msg_size[-8] at 0x4140 = 0x00000000. After sub eax,0x1 and movsxd, the read() size becomes 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF. msg[-8] at 0x4020 = stdout FILE* = 0x00007ffff7fa75c0. read(0, 0x7ffff7fa75c0, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) was called and succeeded.\"},{\"id\":\"E008\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1665\",\"description\":\"Format string at 0x21c4 is '%llu' (unsigned long long), confirming idx is read as unsigned. The comparison at 0x1684 uses jl (signed), creating the signedness mismatch.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm whether the OOB write via edit_cookie can corrupt FILE structures (stdout/stdin/stderr) in a way that affects program behavior beyond crash\",\"Verify which specific global variables and pointers are accessible via different negative idx values\",\"Determine if msg_size OOB read can return non-zero values that limit the write size, or if zero values (leading to -1 size) are the common case\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-002\",\"title\":\"Use-After-Free via eat_cookie compaction (stale duplicate pointer not cleared)\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Use-After-Free\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie frees msg[random_idx] and compacts by moving msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[random_idx]. However, msg[cookie_num-1] is NOT cleared after the copy, leaving a duplicate pointer. If a subsequent eat_cookie call's random_idx equals the current cookie_num-1 (the stale slot), it frees the duplicate pointer. Any other in-range slot that received the same pointer during a prior compaction now holds a dangling pointer to freed memory.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"eat_cookie\",\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg (pointer array at 0x4060)\",\"msg_size (int array at 0x4160)\",\"cookie_num (at 0x41e8)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"Repeated eat_cookie invocations (option 1) — index is from /dev/urandom (not directly attacker-controlled), but attacker controls timing and number of calls\",\"entry_function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"eat_cookie call 1: random_idx=A (A != cookie_num-1). free(msg[A]). msg[A] = msg[cookie_num-1]. msg[cookie_num-1] NOT cleared (duplicate pointer). cookie_num--.\",\"Now msg[A] (in-range) and msg[old_cookie_num-1] (out-of-range) both point to same block P.\",\"eat_cookie call 2: random_idx = new_cookie_num-1 = old_cookie_num-1. free(msg[old_cookie_num-1]) = free(P). msg[old_cookie_num-1] = msg[new_cookie_num-1] (compaction). cookie_num--.\",\"Now msg[A] (still in-range if A < new_cookie_num) holds dangling pointer to freed block P.\",\"edit_cookie(msg[A]) or read_cookie(msg[A]) accesses freed memory → UAF.\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"After moving msg[cookie_num-1] to msg[random_idx], msg[cookie_num-1] should be set to NULL to prevent duplicate/stale pointer\",\"actual\":\"msg[cookie_num-1] is not cleared after the copy, leaving a duplicate pointer that can be freed again by a subsequent eat_cookie call\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing NULL assignment to msg[cookie_num-1] after compaction copy\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"Free of duplicate pointer followed by dereference via in-range slot\",\"address\":\"0x158e (free in eat_cookie), 0x15c6 (msg[idx] = msg[cookie_num-1] copy without clearing source)\",\"expression\":\"free(msg[random_idx]); msg[random_idx] = msg[cookie_num-1]; /* msg[cookie_num-1] not cleared */\",\"target\":\"msg[cookie_num-1] retains duplicate pointer; subsequent free of this slot creates dangling pointer in msg[A]\",\"size\":\"8 bytes (pointer)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"THEORETICAL\",\"reason\":\"If UAF occurs, read_cookie on the dangling pointer slot reads from freed heap memory, potentially leaking freed heap content or metadata.\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"THEORETICAL\",\"reason\":\"If UAF occurs, edit_cookie on the dangling pointer slot writes to freed heap memory via read(0, dangling_ptr, msg_size[idx]-1), corrupting freed heap data.\"},{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"THEORETICAL\",\"reason\":\"Writing to freed heap memory corrupts heap metadata and potentially overlapping allocations.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"THEORETICAL\",\"reason\":\"Dereferencing a dangling pointer after the freed block is reused or unmapped may cause SIGSEGV.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E009\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x15b0-0x15c6\",\"description\":\"Compaction code: 'mov rax, [rcx+rax*1]' loads msg[cookie_num-1], then 'mov QWORD PTR [rcx+rdx*1], rax' stores it to msg[random_idx]. No instruction clears msg[cookie_num-1] (no 'mov QWORD PTR [...], 0' for the source slot).\"},{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x15ff-0x160a\",\"description\":\"cookie_num is decremented ('sub rax,0x1; mov [rip+...],rax') without clearing msg[old_cookie_num-1]. The stale pointer remains in the now-out-of-range slot.\"},{\"id\":\"E011\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x158e\",\"description\":\"GDB trace of 4 successive eat_cookie calls: after call 1 (random_idx=0, cookie_num 5→4), msg[4]=0x555555559580 (not cleared, duplicate of msg[0]=0x555555559580). After call 2 (random_idx=0, cookie_num 4→3), msg[4] still = 0x555555559580 (now freed block). Stale pointer persists across calls.\"},{\"id\":\"E012\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x15c6\",\"description\":\"If call 1 has random_idx=A (A != cookie_num-1), msg[A] and msg[cookie_num-1] both point to same block. If call 2 has random_idx=cookie_num-1, it frees that block. msg[A] (in-range) becomes dangling. This requires random_idx=cookie_num-1 in call 2, probability 1/cookie_num per call. Attacker can retry eat_cookie to achieve this condition.\"}],\"confidence\":\"MEDIUM\",\"verification_required\":[\"Verify the exact probability and feasibility of achieving the UAF condition with repeated eat_cookie calls\",\"Confirm whether the freed block from the UAF scenario is accessible via edit_cookie/read_cookie before reallocation\",\"Determine if heap allocator (glibc 2.39 tcache/fastbin) behavior affects the exploitability of the dangling pointer\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-003\",\"title\":\"NULL pointer dereference in create_cookie with negative message_length\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Signedness Error\",\"description\":\"message_length is read via scanf(%lld) as signed long long. The check 'cmp rax, 0x100; jle' uses signed comparison, so negative values (e.g., -1) pass the check (-1 <= 0x100). calloc() receives the negative value as an unsigned size (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF), which fails and returns NULL. The code then dereferences the NULL pointer when accessing buf[read_return] to check for newline.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"create_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"calloc buffer (stack local [rbp-0x10])\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"message_length via scanf(%lld) in create_cookie\",\"entry_function\":\"create_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"Attacker provides message_length = -1 (or any negative value)\",\"scanf(%lld) stores -1 as 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF in [rbp-0x18]\",\"cmp rax, 0x100; jle passes (-1 <= 0x100 signed)\",\"calloc(0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, 1) returns NULL\",\"read(0, NULL, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) returns -1 or 0\",\"buf[read_return] dereferences NULL + read_return → SIGSEGV\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"message_length should be checked for non-negative values, or comparison should be unsigned (jbe instead of jle)\",\"actual\":\"message_length checked with jle (signed), allowing negative values to pass\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing lower-bound check for message_length (should be >= 0 or >= 1)\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"NULL pointer dereference\",\"address\":\"0x1458\",\"expression\":\"movzx eax, [rax] where rax = NULL_buf + read_return\",\"target\":\"NULL pointer (calloc return for negative size)\",\"size\":\"1 byte read\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"Negative message_length causes calloc to return NULL, and subsequent buf[read_return] access dereferences NULL, causing SIGSEGV. Dynamically verified: message_length=-1 caused SIGSEGV at 0x1458.\"},{\"type\":\"DOS\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The crash terminates the process, denying service to legitimate users.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E013\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x13f6-0x13fc\",\"description\":\"Size check: 'cmp rax, 0x100; jle 0x140a'. jle is signed comparison, so negative message_length values pass the check.\"},{\"id\":\"E014\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1413-0x1416\",\"description\":\"calloc called with rdi=message_length (negative value as unsigned). For -1, rdi=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, calloc fails and returns NULL.\"},{\"id\":\"E015\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1455-0x1458\",\"description\":\"'add rax, rdx; movzx eax, [rax]' where rax=NULL (calloc return) and rdx=read_return. Dereferences NULL+read_return.\"},{\"id\":\"E016\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1458\",\"description\":\"GDB: with message_length=-1, rax=0xffffffffffffffff at 0x140a. calloc returned NULL (rdi=0x0 at 0x1416). SIGSEGV at 0x1458 when accessing buf[read_return].\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm whether message_length=0 also causes issues (calloc(0) may return non-NULL but read(0,buf,0) returns 0, then buf[0] is accessed on a 0-size allocation)\"]}],\"non_vulnerabilities\":[{\"function\":\"main\",\"description\":\"Menu choice is read via scanf(%lld) and compared with exact values 1-5 using cmp/jne. No vulnerability in the dispatch logic.\",\"reason\":\"Choice values are directly compared against constants; no array indexing or memory operation depends on the choice value.\"},{\"function\":\"init\",\"description\":\"Initializes 5 cookies via strdup and sets cookie_num=5. Opens /dev/urandom. All operations are safe.\",\"reason\":\"No attacker-controlled input reaches init(). strdup returns valid heap pointers. urandom NULL check is present.\"},{\"function\":\"menu\",\"description\":\"Prints menu options via puts(). No input processing.\",\"reason\":\"No attacker-controlled data is processed.\"},{\"function\":\"_abort\",\"description\":\"Prints error message and exits. No input processing.\",\"reason\":\"No attacker-controlled data is processed.\"}],\"unresolved\":[{\"description\":\"Whether the eat_cookie UAF (BUG-002) can be reliably triggered by repeated eat_cookie calls to achieve the required random_idx sequence\",\"reason\":\"The random index comes from /dev/urandom and is not attacker-controlled. The probability depends on cookie_num and the specific sequence needed.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Statistical analysis of trigger probability across multiple eat_cookie calls, or runtime testing with repeated calls to observe if the UAF condition is achievable in practice\"},{\"description\":\"Whether the OOB write via edit_cookie (BUG-001) with idx=-8 writing to stdout FILE structure causes observable corruption beyond a crash\",\"reason\":\"The write target is the stdout FILE* structure. The exact impact depends on which fields are overwritten and whether the program continues to use stdout afterward.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Dynamic verification of which FILE structure fields are corrupted and whether the corruption affects subsequent I/O operations\"},{\"description\":\"Whether negative idx values other than -8 access useful targets (e.g., idx=-4 accesses stderr, idx=-1 accesses memory just before msg)\",\"reason\":\"Different negative indices access different global variables. A systematic mapping of accessible targets would clarify the full attack surface.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Enumerate all accessible OOB targets for negative idx values from -1 to -8 (and beyond) and assess each for security impact\"}]},\"primitive_reports\":[{\"schema\":\"PrimitiveReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"stage\":\"S3\",\"target_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"analyzed_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"total_primitives\":3,\"verified\":2,\"theoretical\":0,\"candidate\":1,\"unknown\":0},\"primitives\":[{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-001\",\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Signedness mismatch: idx read via scanf(%llu) as unsigned long long, but bounds check uses jl (signed less-than). Large unsigned values (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8 = -8 signed) pass the check because -8 < 5 in signed comparison. The idx is then used as unsigned 64-bit offset to index into msg[] (8-byte stride), causing OOB access to memory before the array.\",\"description\":\"read_cookie with negative idx loads an OOB pointer from msg[] (e.g., stdout FILE* at 0x4020 for idx=-8) and passes it to printf(%s), leaking memory content at that address.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001\",\"OOB signedness mismatch (jl instead of jb)\",\"msg[idx] with negative idx loads OOB pointer from global variables\",\"printf(%s, msg[idx]) outputs data at OOB pointer target\",\"INFO_LEAK\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Array index (idx) — attacker controls which OOB pointer is loaded from msg[]\",\"how\":\"scanf(%llu) reads idx as unsigned; large values like 18446744073709551608 (-8 signed) bypass signed jl check\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"idx must be a large unsigned value that is negative when interpreted as signed (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8 = -8)\",\"necessity\":\"Required to bypass the signed jl bounds check (idx < cookie_num)\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"msg[idx] must point to readable memory (e.g., stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* structures, __dso_handle)\",\"necessity\":\"printf(%s) dereferences the loaded pointer; if it points to unmapped memory, program crashes instead of leaking\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"The target memory must contain non-null bytes before the first null byte for meaningful leak\",\"necessity\":\"printf(%s) stops at null byte; if first byte is null, nothing is leaked\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-8] = *(0x4060 + (-8)*8) = *(0x4020)\",\"alias\":\"stdout FILE* global variable\",\"implication\":\"read_cookie(idx=-8) loads stdout FILE* and printf outputs FILE structure content (4 bytes before null at offset 4)\"},{\"expression\":\"msg[-6] = *(0x4060 + (-6)*8) = *(0x4030)\",\"alias\":\"stdin FILE* global variable\",\"implication\":\"read_cookie(idx=-6) loads stdin FILE* and printf outputs FILE structure content\"},{\"expression\":\"msg[-4] = *(0x4060 + (-4)*8) = *(0x4040)\",\"alias\":\"stderr FILE* global variable\",\"implication\":\"read_cookie(idx=-4) loads stderr FILE* and printf outputs FILE structure content\"},{\"expression\":\"msg[-11] = *(0x4060 + (-11)*8) = *(0x4008)\",\"alias\":\"__dso_handle (self-referential pointer to PIE base+0x4008)\",\"implication\":\"read_cookie(idx=-11) loads __dso_handle value (a PIE address) and printf outputs 6 bytes of the address, leaking PIE base\"},{\"expression\":\"msg[-13] = *(0x4060 + (-13)*8) = *(0x3ff8)\",\"alias\":\"GOT entry (ld-linux address under Full RELRO)\",\"implication\":\"read_cookie(idx=-13) loads a resolved GOT entry and printf outputs memory at that address\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Dynamic: run read_cookie with idx=-8 and idx=-11, capture printf output, verify leaked bytes match GDB-observed memory content\",\"environment\":\"x86-64 Linux, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled, ASLR on\",\"run_command\":\"python3 /work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"PRIM-001: leaked 4 bytes (8728adfb) from stdout FILE structure via idx=-8\",\"PRIM-002: leaked 6 bytes of __dso_handle address via idx=-11, yielding PIE base\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes base address, but the OOB read leaks the PIE base via __dso_handle (idx=-11), defeating PIE for this primitive\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO makes GOT read-only, preventing GOT overwrite. Does not affect the read primitive (GOT entries are still readable)\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"NX does not affect info leak primitives\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Canary does not affect OOB read on global arrays (not stack-based)\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17aa-0x17ad\",\"description\":\"cmp rdx,rax; jl 0x17bb — idx (rdx, from scanf %llu) compared with cookie_num (rax) using signed jl, allowing large unsigned idx values to pass\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bf-0x17ce\",\"description\":\"lea rdx,[rax*8+0x0]; lea rax,[rip+0x2892]; mov rax,[rdx+rax*1] — idx used as unsigned 64-bit offset (rax*8) to compute msg[idx] address, loading OOB pointer\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17d2-0x17e1\",\"description\":\"mov rsi,rax; lea rdi,[rip+0x9cd]; call printf — OOB pointer passed to printf(%s) as string argument\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bb\",\"description\":\"GDB: with idx=18446744073709551608 (-8), breakpoint at 0x17bb hit. rdx=0xfffffffffffffff8, rax=0x5. jl check passed (-8 < 5 signed). msg[-8] loaded from 0x4020 = stdout FILE* = 0x7ffff7fa75c0. printf output 4 bytes: 87 28 ad fb (stdout FILE _flags field low bytes)\"},{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bb\",\"description\":\"GDB: with idx=18446744073709551605 (-11), msg[-11] loaded from 0x4008 = __dso_handle = 0x555555558008 (self-referential). printf output 6 bytes: 08 80 55 55 55 55 (PIE address bytes, null at byte 7)\"},{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"PoC run: read_cookie(idx=-8) leaked 4 bytes (8728adfb) matching GDB observation of stdout FILE _flags=0xfbad2887. read_cookie(idx=-11) leaked 6 bytes yielding PIE base 0x000057813ee73000\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"8728adfb (stdout FILE flags via idx=-8); 0870e73e8157 -> PIE base 0x57813ee73000 (via idx=-11)\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"PRIM-001: read_cookie(idx=-8) -> printf(%s, stdout_FILE*) -> leaked 4 bytes: 87 28 ad fb (stdout _flags field 0xfbad2887 low bytes). PRIM-002: read_cookie(idx=-11) -> printf(%s, __dso_handle) -> leaked 6 bytes: PIE address 0x57813ee77008, PIE base = 0x57813ee73000. Both verified via GDB memory inspection confirming leaked bytes match FILE structure and __dso_handle content.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"INFO_LEAK is the highest-level read primitive achievable from this BUG. The OOB read can access any global variable before msg[] (0x4060) by choosing appropriate negative idx. Key targets: stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* (idx=-8/-6/-4), __dso_handle (idx=-11, leaks PIE base), GOT entries (idx=-13+, leak ld/libc addresses). printf(%s) null-byte truncation limits each leak to bytes before the first null. Multiple reads with different idx values can leak different regions.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-002\",\"type\":\"RELATIVE_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Signedness mismatch: idx read via scanf(%llu) as unsigned long long, but bounds check uses jl (signed less-than). Large unsigned values pass the check. edit_cookie then uses idx to load OOB pointer from msg[] and OOB size from msg_size[], calling read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1).\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie with negative idx loads an OOB pointer from msg[] (e.g., stdout FILE* at 0x4020 for idx=-8) and calls read(0, OOB_pointer, msg_size[idx]-1), attempting to write attacker data to the OOB pointer target.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001\",\"OOB signedness mismatch (jl instead of jb)\",\"msg[idx] with negative idx loads OOB pointer (stdout/stdin/stderr FILE*)\",\"msg_size[idx] with negative idx loads OOB size (0 or heap ptr low bytes)\",\"read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1) attempts write to OOB pointer target\",\"RELATIVE_WRITE (path reached, write fails)\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Array index (idx) — controls which OOB pointer is loaded as write target; write content is attacker-controlled via stdin\",\"how\":\"scanf(%llu) reads idx as unsigned; large values bypass signed jl check. read(0, ...) reads attacker data from stdin.\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"idx must be a large unsigned value that is negative when interpreted as signed (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8 = -8)\",\"necessity\":\"Required to bypass the signed jl bounds check (idx < cookie_num)\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"msg[idx] must point to writable memory (e.g., stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* structures in libc data segment)\",\"necessity\":\"read() writes to the loaded pointer; if NULL or unmapped, read returns -1 (EFAULT)\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"msg_size[idx] must be a small positive integer so that msg_size[idx]-1 is a valid read size that passes kernel access_ok check\",\"necessity\":\"When msg_size[idx]=0, size becomes -1 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) -> read fails. When msg_size[idx] is a large heap pointer low 4 bytes (~1.4GB), buf+size exceeds user space -> access_ok fails. Only small positive values allow read to succeed.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-04\",\"description\":\"No single negative idx simultaneously satisfies: msg[idx] = valid non-NULL pointer AND msg_size[idx] = small positive integer\",\"necessity\":\"For even negative idx: msg[idx]=valid FILE* but msg_size[idx]=0 or large. For odd negative idx: msg[idx]=NULL but msg_size[idx]=small (0x00005555). The stride mismatch (8 vs 4) prevents alignment.\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-8] = *(0x4160 + (-8)*4) = *(0x4140)\",\"alias\":\"Low 4 bytes of msg[28] (heap pointer low dword)\",\"implication\":\"When msg[28] is filled (24 cookies created), msg_size[-8] = heap_ptr & 0xFFFFFFFF ≈ 0x555598af (1.4GB). read(0, stdout, 0x555598af) fails because stdout_addr + 0x555598af exceeds user space limit.\"},{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-7] = *(0x4160 + (-7)*4) = *(0x4144)\",\"alias\":\"High 4 bytes of msg[28] (heap pointer high dword = 0x00005555)\",\"implication\":\"msg_size[-7] = 0x00005555 = 21845 (small positive!). But msg[-7] = *(0x4028) = 0 (NULL, gap between stdout and stdin). read(0, NULL, 21844) fails with EFAULT.\"},{\"expression\":\"Stride mismatch: msg uses 8-byte stride, msg_size uses 4-byte stride\",\"alias\":\"For any negative idx, msg[idx] and msg_size[idx] access different memory regions that never align to give both valid pointer and small size\",\"implication\":\"The write primitive cannot succeed from this bug alone — no negative idx gives both a valid write target and a passing read size\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Dynamic: GDB breakpoint at edit_cookie read() call site (0x16de), verify read args (fd=0, buf=stdout_FILE*, size), check read return value and stdout FILE structure before/after\",\"environment\":\"x86-64 Linux, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled, ASLR on\",\"run_command\":\"python3 /work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"PRIM-003: edit_cookie(idx=-8) calls read(0, stdout_FILE*, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) -> returns -1\",\"GDB: read args rdi=0, rsi=stdout_addr, rdx=size; read return=-1; stdout unchanged\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes addresses but does not prevent the OOB write path from being reached. The write target (FILE* globals) is loaded from fixed offsets relative to PIE base.\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO makes GOT read-only, preventing GOT overwrite via this primitive. FILE* structures in libc data segment are still writable.\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"NX does not affect write primitives targeting data structures\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Canary does not affect OOB write on global arrays (not stack-based)\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E007\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1681-0x1684\",\"description\":\"cmp rdx,rax; jl 0x1692 — idx (rdx, from scanf %llu) compared with cookie_num (rax) using signed jl, allowing large unsigned idx values to pass\"},{\"id\":\"E008\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16a3-0x16de\",\"description\":\"lea rdx,[rax*4+0x0] for msg_size[idx]; mov eax,[rdx+rax] loads OOB size; sub eax,0x1; movsxd rdx,eax sign-extends to 64-bit; lea rcx,[rax*8+0x0] for msg[idx]; mov rax,[rcx+rax] loads OOB pointer; call read(0, OOB_ptr, OOB_size-1)\"},{\"id\":\"E009\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"GDB: with idx=-8 (no cookies created), read args: rdi=0 (fd), rsi=0x7ffff7fa75c0 (stdout FILE*), rdx=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (size=-1 from msg_size[-8]=0). read returned -1 (EFAULT). stdout FILE structure unchanged.\"},{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"GDB: with idx=-8 (24 cookies created, msg[28] filled), read args: rdi=0, rsi=0x7ffff7fa75c0 (stdout FILE*), rdx=0x555598af (msg_size[-8]=heap ptr low 4 bytes). read returned -1 (access_ok failed: stdout_addr + 0x555598af exceeds user space). stdout FILE structure unchanged.\"},{\"id\":\"E011\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"GDB: with idx=-7 (24 cookies created), read args: rdi=0, rsi=0x0 (NULL, msg[-7]=gap between stdout and stdin), rdx=0x5554 (msg_size[-7]=heap ptr high 4 bytes=0x00005555, size=21844). read returned -1 (EFAULT, NULL buffer).\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"PRIM-003: edit_cookie(idx=-8) reached the OOB write path: read(0, stdout_FILE*, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) was called. GDB confirmed read args: fd=0, buf=0x7ffff7fa75c0 (stdout FILE*), size=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF. read returned -1 (EFAULT). stdout FILE structure unchanged (0x00000000fbad2887 before and after). With 24 cookies created (msg[28] filled): size=0x555598af (heap ptr low bytes), read also returned -1 (access_ok failed). No write succeeded. STATUS: CANDIDATE — write path confirmed but write fails due to size constraints.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"The OOB write path is confirmed reachable: edit_cookie(idx=-8) loads stdout FILE* as write target and calls read(). However, the write never succeeds because: (1) when msg_size[-8]=0, size becomes -1 -> EFAULT; (2) when msg_size[-8]=heap_ptr_low (large), buf+size exceeds user space -> access_ok fails; (3) for odd idx where msg_size is small (0x00005555), msg[idx]=NULL -> EFAULT. The stride mismatch (msg=8 bytes, msg_size=4 bytes) prevents any single negative idx from giving both a valid pointer and a small size. This is a CANDIDATE, not VERIFIED, because no actual memory write was observed.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-003\",\"type\":\"RELATIVE_READ\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Same signedness mismatch as PRIM-001. read_cookie with negative idx loads OOB pointer from msg[] and passes to printf(%s), reading memory at the OOB pointer target.\",\"description\":\"read_cookie with negative idx reads memory at OOB pointer targets. The read is restricted by printf(%s) null-byte termination, limiting each read to bytes before the first null in the target memory.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001\",\"OOB signedness mismatch (jl instead of jb)\",\"msg[idx] with negative idx loads OOB pointer\",\"printf(%s, msg[idx]) reads and outputs memory at OOB pointer\",\"RELATIVE_READ (restricted by null-byte termination)\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"Array index (idx) — controls which OOB pointer is loaded, determining the read target\",\"how\":\"scanf(%llu) reads idx as unsigned; large values bypass signed jl check\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"idx must be a large unsigned value that is negative when interpreted as signed\",\"necessity\":\"Required to bypass the signed jl bounds check\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"msg[idx] must point to readable memory\",\"necessity\":\"printf dereferences the pointer; unmapped memory causes SIGSEGV\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"Target memory must have non-null bytes for meaningful output\",\"necessity\":\"printf(%s) stops at first null byte\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-8] = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE* -> _IO_2_1_stdout_\",\"alias\":\"stdout FILE structure in libc data segment\",\"implication\":\"printf(%s, stdout_FILE*) reads 4 bytes of FILE structure (flags field 0xfbad2887, null at byte 5)\"},{\"expression\":\"msg[-11] = *(0x4008) = __dso_handle (self-referential PIE address)\",\"alias\":\"__dso_handle global variable\",\"implication\":\"printf(%s, __dso_handle) reads 6 bytes of the PIE address (null at byte 7), leaking PIE base\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Dynamic: run read_cookie with idx=-8 and idx=-11, capture printf output, verify against GDB memory dumps\",\"environment\":\"x86-64 Linux, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled\",\"run_command\":\"python3 /work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"Leaked 4 bytes from stdout FILE via idx=-8\",\"Leaked 6 bytes (PIE address) via idx=-11\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes base, but idx=-11 leaks PIE base via __dso_handle, defeating PIE for subsequent operations\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Does not affect read primitive; GOT entries are readable\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Does not affect read primitive\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Does not affect OOB read on global arrays\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E012\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"PoC: read_cookie(idx=-8) leaked 4 bytes: 87 28 ad fb. GDB confirmed stdout FILE at 0x7ffff7fa75c0 contains 87 28 ad fb 00 00 00 00 (flags=0xfbad2887, null at offset 4)\"},{\"id\":\"E013\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"PoC: read_cookie(idx=-11) leaked 6 bytes: 08 70 e7 3e 81 57. GDB confirmed __dso_handle at 0x4008 contains self-referential PIE address 0x57813ee77008 (null at byte 7). PIE base = 0x57813ee73000.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"8728adfb (4 bytes from stdout FILE via idx=-8); 0870e73e8157 (6 bytes PIE address via idx=-11)\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"PRIM-001/PRIM-003: read_cookie(idx=-8) -> printf(%s, stdout_FILE*) -> leaked 4 bytes (87 28 ad fb) = stdout _flags field. read_cookie(idx=-11) -> printf(%s, __dso_handle) -> leaked 6 bytes = PIE address 0x57813ee77008, PIE base = 0x57813ee73000. Both verified via GDB memory inspection. The read is RELATIVE (restricted to specific OOB targets determined by idx) and RESTRICTED (printf %s null-byte termination limits each read).\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"This primitive is merged with PRIM-001 (INFO_LEAK) as they share the same BUG, same preconditions, and same operation (read_cookie OOB read via printf). The distinction is: INFO_LEAK focuses on leaking sensitive information (PIE base, FILE structure content), while RELATIVE_READ describes the general OOB read capability. Both are VERIFIED with the same dynamic evidence. The read is restricted by printf(%s) null-byte termination — each read stops at the first null byte in the target memory.\"}],\"non_primitives\":[{\"primitive_id\":\"NP-001\",\"candidate_type\":\"ARB_WRITE\",\"source_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"reason\":\"ARB_WRITE requires attacker-controlled write target address. For BUG-001, the write target (msg[idx]) comes from fixed global variables (stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* at fixed offsets from msg base). The attacker controls idx but not the pointer values stored at those locations. The pointers are set by libc initialization and cannot be modified by the attacker through this bug alone. Additionally, the write never succeeds due to size constraints (msg_size[idx] is either 0, large, or the pointer is NULL). Therefore ARB_WRITE is not achievable.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"NP-002\",\"candidate_type\":\"ARB_READ\",\"source_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"reason\":\"ARB_READ requires attacker-controlled read target address. For BUG-001, the read target (msg[idx]) comes from fixed global variables. While the attacker can choose which global to read (by selecting idx), the target addresses are not arbitrary — they are limited to the specific global variables before msg[] (stdout/stdin/stderr FILE*, __dso_handle, GOT entries). The attacker cannot specify an arbitrary address Y to read. Therefore this is RELATIVE_READ, not ARB_READ.\"}],\"unresolved\":[{\"description\":\"Whether the RELATIVE_WRITE path can be made to succeed by controlling msg_size[idx] through a separate primitive (e.g., heap manipulation to place a small positive value at the msg_size alias location)\",\"reason\":\"The write fails because msg_size[idx] is either 0 (initial state), a large heap pointer low 4 bytes (after creating cookies), or the write target is NULL. A separate primitive that can write a small positive integer to the msg_size alias location (e.g., msg[28] low 4 bytes for idx=-8) could enable the write.\",\"required_next_step\":\"S4 analysis: combine with BUG-002 (UAF) or BUG-003 (NULL deref) to control msg_size alias values, enabling the RELATIVE_WRITE to succeed\"}]}],\"target_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"bug_reports\":[],\"impact_reports\":[],\"strategy_reports\":[],\"final_exploit_path\":\"\",\"final_report\":{}}",
